# Carrier Wound #36: The Platform Hardcoded a Cartridge's Horizon Model — and Knew It Wrong. A Phantom Config Key No Schema Declares.

Wound #35 cleared the meta-verifier's throat — the oversized verifier prompt
finally rode `@file` to pi instead of E2BIG'ing on argv. So the next live drive
got *past* the verifier and onto the post-execution proof recorder. And there,
waiting since `759a87b`, was a check that could never pass.

The deployed generated-workflow proof went terminal:

```
run-live-... → blocked:
  generated-workflow proof failed check "plan:horizon-coverage":
  "Generated workflow does not cover required memory horizons …"
```

The planner reached `captured`. All generated nodes executed. The execution
proof was clean. The block came from the **platform's own** proof recorder
asserting a horizon-coverage invariant against the generated plan — and that
assertion was structurally unsatisfiable. It had been unsatisfiable for dozens
of commits; it was simply unreachable until #35 removed the failure that fired
first. A latent landmine, armed long ago, stepped on only once the path upstream
of it was finally cleared.

## The wound: the platform reached into a cartridge's domain — and got it wrong

`src/app/workflow-nodes/generated-workflow-proof.ts` — *platform* code, shared by
every cartridge — carried a hardcoded `plan:horizon-coverage` check. It read each
step's `config["memoryCoverageHorizons"]`: a plural-array key that **no node
config schema in the repo declares.** Not the dream memory nodes, not the
support-sweep signal-search node. A phantom.

Two cartridges, two distinct ways the phantom misfired:

- **Dream (structurally unsatisfiable).** In the memory fabric, a horizon is a
  runtime *output* — `horizonFor` buckets each captured signal into `24h / 7d /
  30d / quarter / all-time` at execution time. It is never a *config input*; no
  dream planning step declares horizons, because horizons aren't a thing the
  planner chooses. So the platform check read `memoryCoverageHorizons` off every
  dream step, found `undefined` every time, computed "covered: none," and failed.
  There is no plan a Dream planner could emit that satisfies it. It demanded a
  config key for a value the domain produces, not consumes.
- **Support-sweep (miswired).** Here horizons *are* a config input — but the real
  field on `aihero.support-sweep.signal-search` is `horizons` (singular usage),
  defaulting to all five. The platform read `memoryCoverageHorizons` — the wrong
  name — so it saw `undefined` here too and would fail a plan that genuinely
  searches every horizon.

The class is **information leakage**. The "horizon model" is a design decision —
which horizons exist, what the config field is named, what the default coverage
is — and that decision lived in *two* places: the cartridge's node config schema
(`AiHeroSignalSearchNodeConfigSchema`, which got it right: `horizons`, all-five
default) and the platform's proof check (which got it wrong: `memoryCoverageHorizons`,
no default). Ousterhout's back-door leakage, the pernicious kind, because it
isn't in any interface — two modules silently depending on the same concept, and
when they disagree on its details, one of them is simply broken.

> "The opposite of information hiding is information leakage. Information leakage
> occurs when a design decision is reflected in multiple modules. This creates a
> dependency between the modules… Back-door leakage like this is more pernicious
> than leakage through an interface, because it isn't obvious. Information leakage
> is one of the most important red flags in software design… ask yourself 'How
> can I reorganize these classes so that this particular piece of knowledge only
> affects a single class?'"
> — John Ousterhout, *A Philosophy of Software Design*, §Information Leakage

The platform never should have known what a horizon is. Horizons are a property
of *retrieval-shaped* cartridges; the memory fabric has them as outputs,
support-sweep has them as inputs, and a third cartridge might have none. A
platform that hardcodes one cartridge's domain concept has leaked that concept
across the boundary — and here it leaked a *corrupted copy*, a key spelled
differently than any schema, so the leak failed loud the moment it became
reachable.

## The fix: pull the knowledge out of the platform so it affects a single module

Ousterhout's prescription verbatim: reorganize so the horizon knowledge affects
exactly one module. The platform (`generated-workflow-proof.ts`) now carries
**zero** horizon model — the `plan:horizon-coverage` check and its
`memoryCoverageHorizons` read are gone. Horizon coverage is relocated entirely to
a **cartridge-injected** check through the proof recorder's existing
`buildAdditionalProofChecks` hook, which already threads `{ executionProof, plan,
planArtifact }` to each cartridge:

- **Support-sweep injects** `buildAiHeroSupportSweepHorizonCoverageProofCheck`
  (`src/cartridges/aihero-support-sweep/horizon-coverage-proof-check.ts`), reading
  its own real `horizons` field.
- **Dream injects nothing** — the memory-fabric installer destructures only
  `executionProof` and adds no horizon check, because in that cartridge horizons
  are an output and there is nothing to assert at plan time.

The knowledge now lives in exactly one place per cartridge. Change the horizon
set, change one module. The platform stays a deep, horizon-agnostic module that
runs whatever checks a cartridge hands it.

### Read EFFECTIVE horizons — executor-faithful, not raw planner output

The relocated check does **not** read raw `step.config`. It parses each
signal-search step config THROUGH `AiHeroSignalSearchNodeConfigSchema.safeParse`
— the exact schema the executor parses with — so the `.default(["24h","7d",
"30d","quarter","all-time"])` applies precisely as it does at run time. This is
the whole point: the check must judge what the run *actually searches*, not what
the planner literally typed.

- **Omitted `horizons`** (`{ axes, maxSignals, query }`) → schema default → all
  five → covered, PASS. The planner never needs to emit the key; omission means
  "search everything," which is correct, so the check passes. *This is the exact
  case the old phantom check could never pass.*
- **Narrowed** (`{ horizons: ["24h"] }`) → covered `["24h"]`, missing the rest →
  FAIL, naming the missing horizons. A genuine collapse into recent-only
  retrieval, genuinely caught.
- **Garbage merged token** (`{ horizons: ["quarter-all-time"] }`) →
  `filterToValidHorizons` drops the invalid token, returns `undefined`, schema
  applies its default → all five → PASS. A planner typo can't fabricate a
  narrowing that the executor wouldn't actually honor.
- **Union across steps** (`["24h","7d"]` + `["30d","quarter","all-time"]`) →
  covered union = all five → PASS.

### Legibility: the summary IS the operator-facing blocker

`memoryGeneratedWorkflowProofBlocker` joins failed checks' `summary` strings
verbatim, so the check's `summary` names *required*, *covered*, and *missing*
horizons in every branch. A narrowed plan reads "Required: 24h, 7d, 30d, quarter,
all-time; covered: 24h; missing: 7d, 30d, quarter, all-time" — the operator
diagnoses from the blocker without opening the check.

> "A good failure message contains much the same information as the test's name:
> it should clearly express the desired outcome, the actual outcome, and any
> relevant parameters… 'Expected an account in state CLOSED, but got account
> <{name: "my-account", state: "OPEN"}>'"
> — *Software Engineering at Google*, §Write Clear Failure Messages

## The chaos test that would have caught it (hostile shapes, not polite mocks)

`tests/integration/aihero-support-sweep-cartridge.test.ts` grows a new
`describe("… horizon-coverage proof check (hostile planner shapes)")`. It builds
**real** support-sweep plans — real `signal-search` steps, real `dependsOn`, real
`PlanArtifact` — and runs them through the **real**
`buildAiHeroSupportSweepHorizonCoverageProofCheck`, which parses configs through
the **real** `AiHeroSignalSearchNodeConfigSchema`. Six cases cover the four
effective-horizon branches above plus the no-signal-search-step case
(`toBeFalsy` + "declares no") and the checkId/required-list contract.

The keystone is the **omitted-horizons → PASS** case. That is precisely the
false negative the old platform check produced: a plan that genuinely searches
all five horizons (by omitting the key and taking the default) was failed by a
check demanding a phantom `memoryCoverageHorizons` key. The new check passes it
because it reads what the executor reads. The dream side is guarded in
`tests/integration/workflow-app.test.ts`: the generated-workflow proof now reaches
`verified` with **no** horizon check in its check-id set at all — the platform no
longer asserts a horizon model against a cartridge that has none.

This is a `[[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]` instance, textbook. The old tests
fed the check `memoryCoverageHorizons` directly — they handed the check the very
phantom key it wanted, so they passed *green* while every production planner
(which emits `horizons`, or omits it) would have the check fail *red*. The test
double was politer than production: it spoke the check's private vocabulary
instead of the schema's real one. The antidote is the hostile double — feed the
check what real planners actually emit (`horizons`, or nothing), through the real
schema, and watch the old check's logic fail the very plans it was meant to bless.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · full suite **439 passed / 55 files**
(six new hostile cases over the prior 433).

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

This removes a mis-generalized platform check and relocates horizon coverage to
the one cartridge that owns the concept. It proves, in-suite, that the support-sweep
check passes genuinely-covered plans and fails genuinely-narrowed ones, and that
the dream proof path no longer carries a horizon assertion. It does **not**, by
itself, prove the dream run reaches `captured` — only a live re-drive on the
deployed fix does. The hypothesis is concrete and falsifiable: the dream proof
recorder failed because the platform asserted an unsatisfiable horizon invariant;
with that invariant removed from the platform and absent from the dream cartridge,
the generated-workflow proof reaches `verified` and the run proceeds to `captured`.
If instead a *different* proof check now blocks, that is a new, distinct signal —
not this one assumed away. One fresh deploy carries the change; the next live
drive says which.

## The carrier discipline (sixteenth carrier wound)

#34 fixed *what you can see when the verifier says nothing.* #35 fixed *whether
the verifier could speak at all.* #36 fixes *a check that, once the verifier
finally spoke, refused every answer.* The class is not "horizon coverage was
wrong" — it's "**the platform encoded a domain concept that belongs to a
cartridge, encoded it wrong (a phantom key matching no schema), and hid the
defect behind an earlier failure until that failure was cleared.**" The fix is
Ousterhout's information-hiding as a carrier invariant: a platform module must not
know a cartridge's domain decisions; relocate the knowledge so it affects exactly
one module, and read *effective* config (through the executor's own schema) so the
check judges what the run does, not what the planner typed. A latent
unsatisfiable check is the worst landmine of all — it costs nothing until the day
the path reaches it, and then it fails a system that was, by every other measure,
working.
